 new residents are being accepted  again  callisto tavares worries we haven't done  enough to avoid more unnecessary death  this is still happening there's still  people crying out for help and there's  no one there to help and why the need  for lasting solutions in these homes  remains as urgent as ever jamie morocco  global news toronto  the high cost of housing coming up the  struggle to keep up with the demand for  homes as the pandemic heats up the real  estate market  there's been a real estate boom in many  parts of canada including here in nova  scotia low interest rates and so many  people working from home has created  huge demand and there's a housing  shortage in this province as ross lord  explains that's led to some house  hunting strategies that are unorthodox  and ugly  it's a fact of life in many of canada's  biggest cities vancouver  toronto  montreal  housing demand exceeding available  supply but it's a growing reality in  halifax too leaving more people priced  out whether they're looking to buy or  rent so i started out looking at  different buildings just look them up on  google find their website and their  phone number call them see if they have  anything and  everybody called me back and said we got  nothing that's how john barrett oglevy  ended up here in a hotel room his eight  month lease  hundred dollars a month including  utilities less than what many halifax  landlords charge there's no kitchen but  ogilvy isn't complaining concession is  probably a good word um i've kind of  resigned myself to it right like i was  excited to finally get an apartment look  for a place of my own and now this is  kind of screwed it over but  what can you do to slow down runaway  rent increases nova scotia currently has  a cap in place of 2 percent a year but  the new premier  says he plans to remove the cap  it's not the solution so we really need  more housing stock in this province  that's how we'll solve the housing  crisis  the price of houses has also escalated  in part from people moving to nova  scotia during the pandemic some to find  peace of mind  others hoping to make their dollar go  further but no such luck i had a  property that i had 32 offers on i mean  that's  unheard of  for young nova scotians sky high prices  are a downer it seems that there is very  limited ways or even prospect to like  become a homeowner  some blame the housing crisis for an  increase in temporary homeless shelters  like this one that was forcibly removed  by halifax regional police last month  the problem is now spilling over into  small town nova scotia where employers  say housing shortages are part of the  reason they can't attract enough workers  we're currently looking at options to  buy housing or to build housing  many private businesses locally have  already gone that route and have  made the investment in employee housing  for their businesses the real estate  industry itself is calling for the  government to waive the provincial  transfer taxes saving home buyers  thousands of dollars but there's no sign  of immediate relief to make finding a  decent place to live more affordable  ross lord global news halifax  still ahead an example of the family  doctor so many canadians are searching  for  one of the biggest issues for voters  here in nova scotia and across the  country is health care in particular  access to a family doctor the canadian  medical association estimates about 5  million canadians don't have a primary  care physician that has a trickle-down  effect from late diagnoses to a heavy  reliance on hospital emergency  departments  we went up the road from here to meet a  family doctor who has found a way to  make things work for people in his  community perfect you could call his  approach old school you know the heart's  sounding good there michelle dr michelle  chiasson is the kind of family doctor  tens of thousands of canadians long for  you get to know your patients you get  really meaningful relationships you get  to know the grandparents the parents and  the children the whole families primary  care physicians family doctors are you  know the foundation of health care  instead of a big city practice he chose  cheda camp an acadian fishing village on  the west coast of cape breton island  nova scotia  community is everything from his role as  a hockey coach to the babies he's  delivered  to his rounds at the long-term care home  his connections here are deep we have to  cover the emergency department we cover  the hospital we do our clinics we do  nursing home we do the youth health  center we do home visits you know we're  an important resource to the community  and very happy to be that resource to  the community  he admits it can be daunting at times  sometimes uh you know you are the one  dealing with something at three o'clock  in the morning and there's no there's no  surgeon or specialist down the hall  the key is collaboration he is not alone  he's one of four family doctors here  supported by a team nurses family  practice nurses doctors our ot our  physiotherapist social worker we have a  team that gets together and really cares  for the patients so what i really would  say is to the government that they  really need to focus on putting teams in  place  not just trying to recruit  an extra doctor here they need to look  at all these communities big and small  and realize that medicine is changing  now let's have a look at these thumbs  here right the population is aging the  care they need more complex  we have tons of phone calls every  every week from people  from sydney from glace bay from port  hood who would like to come to shetty  camp  to see a doctor  you know and unfortunately you know if  they're not in their catchment area we  can't see them  it's nice to see you he's a rare breed  part of a team of family doctors  offering the kind of cradle to grave  health care canadians expect  but that is increasingly elusive  it was here in bedec over a century ago  that aviation history was made that  story coming up and this is the kitson  island lighthouse built in 1912.  we're back from vedic nova scotia in a  moment  there's only one way to get across this  great country in a day or so and that's  by airplane it's so easy i take it for  granted and so do our political leaders  who crisscross the country campaigning  but imagine more than 100 years ago when  passenger jets were still a dream seeing  the first powered flight of a plane in  canada well it happened right here in  tiny badeck nova scotia thanks in large  part to the man who also invented the  telephone  alexander graham bell whose genius  helped shape the modern world adored  nova scotia and the stately home he  built in the deck is still standing  best known for inventing the telephone  he was equally obsessed with something  else  flight it was here 112 years ago that  the first powered flight in canada and  the british empire took place it was a  cold february day for door lake frozen  solid was the runway  this was the place canada first entered  the era of flight  it was called the silver dart a delicate  looking contraption that had no brakes  was difficult to maneuver and was made  of steel tubing wires bamboo and wood  men on skates helped steady the wings  and badeck-born pilot john alexander  douglas makurdi gracefully took to the  air mccurdy got inside the aircraft  and he rolled it on the ice for about  maybe 500 feet it uh lifted off the air  got up to about 80 feet and flew for  about  two kilometers among those watching  alexander graham bell he and his wife  mabel were passionate about flight at a  time when most considered it a wild idea  people thought bell was a bit of a nut  bar anybody that was involved in  aviation in those days was considered a  bit out to lunch  even before the wright brothers had  taken off at kitty hawk bell had done  hundreds of experiments with kites and  had assembled a team called the aerial  experiment association its aim was  simple put a man in the air  we breathed an atmosphere of aviation  from morning till night bell said and  almost from night to morning  it was a fun airplane to fly ed lubitz  built a replica of the silver dart this  was a rocket ship of the day i mean this  was a  absolutely cutting cutting-edge  state-of-the-art airplane we spent a lot  of time saying well what would a 1909  mechanical engineer know  and the answer was a lot more than you  would think innovations from that plane  endure today from ailerons the hinged  edges on the wings that control the role  of a plane to the tricycle undercarriage  i'm not going to take anything away from  the historic  implications of the wright brothers but  i would say to you the silver dart is  equally as important  because of the innovation in the  engineering and the scientific  principles they applied as well quite a  legacy for a small cape breton town when  alexander graham bell loved so much he  chose it as his final resting place  and that is global national for this  thursday i'm donna friesen we leave you  tonight with some of the stunning vistas  from cape breton island i'll see you on  tuesday from quebec city as we cross the  country in the countdown to the federal  election thanks for watching bye  [Music]
 

